# Rank 1013 / CVE-2024-1735: Armeria SAML authentication bypass. When the
# binding protocol is HTTP POST, SamlMessageUtil.validateSignature returned
# early for a message carrying no signature:
#
#   // Skip signature validation if the object is not signed.
#   if (!signableObj.isSigned()) {
#       return;
#   }
#
# Returning normally means "valid", so an attacker forges an unsigned SAML
# assertion and authenticates as any user (CWE-304, CWE-347). The fixed
# revision adds a signatureRequired property (default true) and throws
# InvalidSamlRequestException when the signature is absent.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap: no fail-open branch fact. The right concept exists and
# covers this weakness, code.OptionalClientSignatureVerification
# (VYQL-AUTH-072, CWE-287/347): "client authentication verifies a signature only
# when a supplied signature value is non-null". Its only Java binding
# (optionalClientSignatureVerification378, vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql)
# requires raw JCA vocabulary -- Signature.getInstance, initVerify, verify --
# which OpenSAML code never contains, so nothing labels the file at all
# (vyql match on the vulnerable file reports no source, sink or control, and
# full scans of the real checkout report 1556 findings / 1003 at or above high
# on both the vulnerable and the fixed revision with none on SamlMessageUtil).
# The binding layer cannot express the discriminator:
#  - Both revisions call isSigned and getSignature, and both contain a bare
#    return and a throw; only the branch each sits in differs.
#  - The one textual asymmetry inverts: the fixed guard is a binary expression
#    (expr token "signature==null||!signableObj.isSigned()") while the
#    vulnerable guard is a bare unary negation that produces no expr token, so
#    keying on expr reports the patched code and stays silent on the flaw. The
#    same key would also flag the fail-closed form
#    "if (!signableObj.isSigned()) throw ...", which produces no expr token
#    either -- four genuinely-enforcing spellings would fire.
#  - The remaining differences (isSigned now called after getSignature, the
#    signatureRequired parameter, the new message literals) are the fixing
#    commit's own layout.
# The JCA binding's workaround (require "signature!=null", forbid
# "signature==null") does not transfer: the vulnerable function already
# contains "signature == null" in the later check that does throw.
#
# What is missing is a control-flow fact linking a guard condition to whether
# its branch terminates in a throw or in a plain return: the Java function
# token walk has no throw_statement case and a bare "return;" contributes no
# token at all. That one fact would cover a whole family beyond this CVE:
# optional signature verification, optional authorization checks, and verifiers
# that return success on error. It is the same flat-bag-of-text limitation the
# engine-gap record states for the negation-cancellation pattern, and it needs
# the one-time structured-context sweep there, not a per-rank token.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap

test "rank1013: SAML signature validation returns early for an unsigned message"
  lang java
  file saml/src/main/java/com/linecorp/armeria/server/saml/SamlMessageUtil.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-072
  code
  ```
  final class SamlMessageUtil {
      static void validateSignature(Credential validationCredential, SignableSAMLObject signableObj) {
          // Skip signature validation if the object is not signed.
          if (!signableObj.isSigned()) {
              return;
          }

          final Signature signature = signableObj.getSignature();
          if (signature == null) {
              throw new InvalidSamlRequestException("failed to validate a signature because no signature exists");
          }

          try {
              signatureProfileValidator.validate(signature);
              SignatureValidator.validate(signature, validationCredential);
          } catch (SignatureException e) {
              throw new InvalidSamlRequestException("failed to validate a signature", e);
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1013: fix rejects an unsigned message unless signatures are explicitly optional"
  lang java
  file saml/src/main/java/com/linecorp/armeria/server/saml/SamlMessageUtil.java
  reject VYQL-AUTH-072
  code
  ```
  final class SamlMessageUtil {
      static void validateSignature(Credential validationCredential, SignableSAMLObject signableObj,
                                    boolean signatureRequired) {
          // Reject the object if signature is missing, unless allowed explicitly.
          final Signature signature = signableObj.getSignature();
          if (signature == null || !signableObj.isSigned()) {
              if (signatureRequired) {
                  throw new InvalidSamlRequestException("rejecting due to a missing signature");
              } else {
                  return;
              }
          }

          try {
              signatureProfileValidator.validate(signature);
              SignatureValidator.validate(signature, validationCredential);
          } catch (SignatureException e) {
              throw new InvalidSamlRequestException("failed to validate a signature", e);
          }
      }
  }
  ```
